Measured area ratio 0.769800358920; exact ratio 0.769800358920.
\image(figures/ellipsoid_tiny.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The actual cut moves the center left and expands its vertical radius. The dashed line is the central cutting plane; blue encloses the retained gray half.)
\alttext(A gray unit circle and blue shifted ellipse share native equal coordinate scales. A dashed vertical cut is at zero and a red arrow points to the discarded right half.)

> Derive the cut and the conditional objective bound

Write the ellipsoid as E={c+Lu: ||u||<=1}, where L is lower triangular
with positive diagonal. Its mathematical shape matrix is P=LL^T.
Suppose f is convex, g is a valid subgradient at c, and a minimizer x*
is inside E. The subgradient inequality gives

\[
f(x^*)\ge f(c)+g^T(x^*-c),\qquad g^T(x^*-c)\le0.
\]

Thus the central halfspace retains x*. For n>1, putting
v=Pg/sqrt(g^TPg), the enclosing ellipsoid has

\[
c'=c-\frac{v}{n+1},\qquad
P'=\frac{n^2}{n^2-1}\left(P-\frac{2}{n+1}vv^T\right).
\]

In one dimension, the update is interval bisection. The core computes
u=L^Tg/||L^Tg|| and v=Lu, and updates a triangular factor of the
new shape. It never needs to form P or square the support norm. A
scaled projection and hypot-based norm avoid avoidable squaring
underflow and overflow. Checked arithmetic still has finite limits.

For completeness, let b=2/(n+1), s[-1]=1 and
s[j]=1-b sum(k<=j) u[k]^2. The lower factor B of I-buu^T has
B[j,j]=sqrt(s[j]/s[j-1]) and
B[i,j]=-b u[i]u[j]/sqrt(s[j-1]s[j]) for i>j.
Multiplying L by this factor with row suffix sums costs O(n^2).
Every new diagonal is a product of positive terms; subtracting nearly
equal dense shape entries is avoided. The implementation uses extended
intermediates and stores the resulting finite positive factor as doubles.
This is numerical geometry, not an interval-arithmetic enclosure proof.

Cauchy-Schwarz supplies a conditional objective-gap bound:

\[
f(c)-f(x^*)\le g^T(c-x^*)\le \|L^Tg\|.
\]

The tolerance therefore has objective units. It is not a bound on the
step or distance to x*. A zero subgradient certifies a global minimum
for a valid convex oracle. The library cannot check convexity, the
subgradient claim or the initial enclosure: those are caller assumptions.
If an optimum is outside the initial ball, these guarantees do not apply.

> Run the original weighted quadratic through the actual kernel

Use f(x,y)=(x-3)^2+5(y+1)^2 and its exact gradient. Its unique minimizer
(3,-1), with value zero, lies in the initial radius-ten ball. We keep the
original cap of 500 subgradient attempts and absolute tolerance 1e-10.
The trace owns the actual objective values, queried gradients and
committed factors from this same execution. No second recurrence is
used to draw the plot. The best evaluated value is retained because a
new center can have a larger objective than the preceding center.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/ellipsoid_original.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main(){
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");std::size_t fc=0,gc=0;
  auto f=[&](const std::vector<double>& x){++fc;return (x[0]-3)*(x[0]-3)+5*(x[1]+1)*(x[1]+1);};
  auto g=[&](const std::vector<double>& x){++gc;return std::vector<double>{2*(x[0]-3),10*(x[1]+1)};};
  const auto t=dm::algorithms::ellipsoid_minimize_trace(f,g,{0,0},10,500,1e-10);
  const auto& o=t.outcome;
  if(o.stop!=dm::algorithms::EllipsoidStop::support_tolerance||o.result.value>1e-10||fc!=o.work.objective_calls||gc!=o.work.subgradient_calls)throw std::runtime_error("quadratic verification failed");
  std::print("Original quadratic: best ({:.12f},{:.12f}); value {:.12g}; last support {:.12g}; tolerance stop {}.\n",o.result.x[0],o.result.x[1],o.result.value,o.last_support_bound,o.stop==dm::algorithms::EllipsoidStop::support_tolerance);
  std::print("Actual calls: objective {}; subgradient {}; committed cuts {}; captured entries {}.\n\n",fc,gc,o.work.updates,t.captured_entries);
  std::print("| committed cut | current objective | best objective | log(volume / initial) |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  std::vector<double> x,current,best,volume,theory;std::size_t rises=0;
  for(std::size_t i=0;i<t.samples.size();++i){const auto& s=t.samples[i];x.push_back(i);current.push_back(std::log10(std::max(s.objective,1e-16)));best.push_back(std::log10(std::max(s.best_value,1e-16)));volume.push_back(s.log_volume-t.samples[0].log_volume);theory.push_back(i*dm::algorithms::ellipsoid_log_volume_ratio(2));
    if(i&&s.objective>t.samples[i-1].objective)++rises;
    if(i%20==0||i+1==t.samples.size())std::print("| {} | {:.12g} | {:.12g} | {:.9f} |\n",i,s.objective,s.best_value,volume.back());}
  std::print("\nCenter objective increases on {} committed cuts.\n",rises);
  auto a=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();a.line(x,current,"current",{148,163,184}).line(x,best,"best",{37,99,235}).title("Actual quadratic evaluations").x_label("Committed cuts").y_label("log10(objective), display floor 1e-16");
  auto b=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();b.line(x,theory,"exact formula",{220,38,38}).points(x,volume,"measured",{37,99,235},3).title("Measured factor volumes match theory").x_label("Committed cuts").y_label("Natural log(volume / initial)");
  dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1,2).add(a).add(b).size(1100,520).title("Original quadratic: actual objectives and triangular-factor volumes").fit_panels().save_svg("build/ellipsoid_original.svg");
}
```
