\caption(The gray current objective can rise, while the blue best objective never rises. The measured log volumes come from the actual saved diagonals. An objective floor of 1e-16 is used only to draw logarithms.)
\alttext(Two numerical panels show current and best objective values against committed cuts, then actual factor log volumes overlaid with the exact central-cut formula.)

The original dense-shape implementation returned approximately
7.56e-9 on this same quadratic, above the requested 1e-10 tolerance.
An instrumented copy shows that its stop test accepted a negative
g^TPg after its matrix became nonsymmetric with a negative diagonal.
That is a numerical failure, not a convex optimality certificate.
The factored kernel above keeps the full original iteration allowance
and returns an explicit support-tolerance reason. Its best point and
last center are separate outputs; the most recent support belongs to
the queried pre-cut state, which can precede the final state on a
run that reaches its iteration limit.

> One dimension: exact bisection and a nonsmooth minimum

For f(x)=|x-2|, start at zero with radius four. The first subgradient
is -1, so the kept interval is [0,4]. Its new center is exactly two,
and the next subgradient is zero. Both objective calls and both oracle
queries appear in the trace. The original plateau objective
|x-1|+|x+2| is also convex; every x in [-2,1] minimizes it with value
three. Starting at five with radius ten, the first bisection center
is zero, already in this minimizing interval.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/ellipsoid_interval.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main(){
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  auto f=[](const std::vector<double>& x){return std::abs(x[0]-2);};
  auto g=[](const std::vector<double>& x){return std::vector<double>{x[0]<2?-1.:x[0]>2?1.:0.};};
  const auto t=dm::algorithms::ellipsoid_minimize_trace(f,g,{0},4,10);
  std::print("Absolute value: best x {}; value {}; objective calls {}; subgradient calls {}; cuts {}; entries {}.\n\n",t.outcome.result.x[0],t.outcome.result.value,t.outcome.work.objective_calls,t.outcome.work.subgradient_calls,t.outcome.work.updates,t.captured_entries);
  std::print("| actual sample | center | radius | objective | queried gradient | updated |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | :--- |\n");
  dm::graphics::Diagram d(75,1100);dm::graphics::DiagramStroke stroke;stroke.width=5;stroke.color={37,99,235};
  for(std::size_t i=0;i<t.samples.size();++i){const auto& s=t.samples[i];const auto& it=t.iterations.at(i);const double c=s.state.center[0],r=s.state.lower[0][0],y=-2.*i;
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",i,c,r,s.objective,it.gradient[0],it.updated);
    d.path({{c-r,y},{c+r,y}},stroke).text({c-r,y-.45},std::format("{}",c-r),20).text({c+r,y-.45},std::format("{}",c+r),20).label({c,y},std::format("c={}, f={}",c,s.objective),22)
     .text({c,y+.55},std::format("query g={}; update {}",it.gradient[0],it.updated),20);}
  d.text({0,1.7},"Exact one-dimensional central cut",26).save_svg("build/ellipsoid_interval.svg");
  auto pf=[](const std::vector<double>& x){return std::abs(x[0]-1)+std::abs(x[0]+2);};
  auto pg=[](const std::vector<double>& x){return std::vector<double>{(x[0]>1?1.:-1.)+(x[0]>-2?1.:-1.)};};
  const auto p=dm::algorithms::ellipsoid_minimize_work(pf,pg,{5},10,200);
  std::print("\nOriginal nonsmooth plateau: best x {}; value {}; attempted queries {}; cuts {}; zero-subgradient stop {}.\n",p.result.x[0],p.result.value,p.result.iterations,p.work.updates,p.stop==dm::algorithms::EllipsoidStop::zero_subgradient);
}
