\caption(Actual repeated factor cuts agree with the exact volume formula while a perpendicular radius grows. There is no inference from volume alone to Euclidean error.)
\alttext(Two plots compare shrinking and growing log radii, then measured log volumes and the exact ratio across twenty actual pure cuts.)

A convergence theorem needs additional assumptions: an appropriate
initial enclosure, valid oracles and a relation between objective
accuracy and the volume of acceptable points, for example through
Lipschitz and interior-ball bounds. This report makes no universal
conditioning-independent rate claim for arbitrary convex black boxes.
The quadratic's known optimum and observed gap are separate checks.
Explicit convex oracles are the natural inputs here; a real-world
dataset would add no evidence about these geometric identities.

> Work, capture limits and finite numerical boundaries

Each accepted cut costs O(n^2) arithmetic and stores one n by n
factor. Ordinary result/work calls keep O(n^2) state without optional
history. Trace history grows with both committed cuts and oracle
attempts. For dimension n, let S=1+n+n^2 and I=1+n. Its logical
capture count is C=number_of_samples*S+number_of_attempts*I. Every
stored upper-zero factor entry is counted. Sample zero is reserved
before the first objective; I is reserved before a subgradient;
the next S is reserved before the next objective and retained copy.
The default cap is one million entries and zero disables that quota.
Container capacities, runtime state, candidate arithmetic, callback
storage and the separate outcome copy are excluded. C is not peak RAM.
Failures can follow earlier callback side effects but return no partial
trace. Caller exceptions propagate without being recast.

The following valid convex workload, f(x,y)=|x+2| in a radius-four
ball, deliberately uses tolerance zero and finite caps. For these caps
every attempted cut succeeds: objective calls=T+1, lower projection
terms=3T and C=10T+7. These are logical work counts, not timings.
We also test the exact ten-entry one-dimensional run above, a cap
one short, zero iterations, an unsafe oracle shape and a rounded
center that cannot move at its represented scale.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/ellipsoid_limits.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main(){
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  auto f=[](const std::vector<double>& x){return std::abs(x[0]+2);};
  auto g=[](const std::vector<double>& x){return std::vector<double>{x[0]<-2?-1.:x[0]>-2?1.:0.,0};};
  std::vector<double> caps,calls,products,capture;
  std::print("| cap T | accepted cuts | objective calls | subgradient calls | support products | entries |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for(int cap:{1,2,4,8,16,20}){const auto t=dm::algorithms::ellipsoid_minimize_trace(f,g,{0,0},4,cap,0);const auto& w=t.outcome.work;
    if(w.updates!=std::size_t(cap)||t.captured_entries!=std::size_t(10*cap+7))throw std::runtime_error("work identity failed");
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",cap,w.updates,w.objective_calls,w.subgradient_calls,w.support_products,t.captured_entries);caps.push_back(cap);calls.push_back(w.objective_calls);products.push_back(w.support_products);capture.push_back(t.captured_entries);}
  auto qf=[](const std::vector<double>& x){return std::abs(x[0]-2);};
  auto qg=[](const std::vector<double>& x){return std::vector<double>{x[0]<2?-1.:x[0]>2?1.:0.};};
  const auto exact=dm::algorithms::ellipsoid_minimize_trace(qf,qg,{0},4,10,1e-10,10);
  bool short_cap=false,bad_shape=false;int fc=0,gc=0;
  try{(void)dm::algorithms::ellipsoid_minimize_trace([&](const std::vector<double>& x){++fc;return qf(x);},[&](const std::vector<double>& x){++gc;return qg(x);},{0},4,10,1e-10,9);}catch(const std::length_error&){short_cap=true;}
  try{(void)dm::algorithms::ellipsoid_minimize_work(qf,[](const std::vector<double>&){return std::vector<double>{};},{0},4);}catch(const std::invalid_argument&){bad_shape=true;}
  const auto none=dm::algorithms::ellipsoid_minimize_trace(qf,qg,{0},4,0,1e-10,3);
  const auto stalled=dm::algorithms::ellipsoid_minimize_work([](const std::vector<double>& x){return x[0];},[](const std::vector<double>&){return std::vector<double>{1};},{1e100},1,10,0);
  std::print("\nExact entries {}; one-short rejected {}; prior objective/subgradient calls {}/{}; wrong gradient shape rejected {}.\n",exact.captured_entries,short_cap,fc,gc,bad_shape);
  std::print("Zero cap: objective calls {}; subgradient calls {}; bound present {}; iteration-limit reason {}.\n",none.outcome.work.objective_calls,none.outcome.work.subgradient_calls,none.outcome.has_support_bound,none.outcome.stop==dm::algorithms::EllipsoidStop::iteration_limit);
  std::print("Numeric probe: center stagnation {}; accepted cuts {}; positive support {}; this linear probe asserts no enclosed optimum.\n",stalled.stop==dm::algorithms::EllipsoidStop::center_stagnation,stalled.work.updates,stalled.last_support_bound>0);
  auto a=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();a.line(caps,calls,"objective T+1",{37,99,235}).points(caps,calls,"",{37,99,235},5).line(caps,products,"products 3T",{22,163,74}).title("Actual oracle and projection work").x_label("Attempt cap T").y_label("Logical calls or terms");
  auto b=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();b.line(caps,capture,"actual C=10T+7",{124,58,237}).points(caps,capture,"",{124,58,237},5).title("Checked optional history").x_label("Attempt cap T").y_label("Logical retained entries");
  dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1,2).add(a).add(b).size(1100,520).title("Valid convex workload: arithmetic work and optional history are distinct").fit_panels().save_svg("build/ellipsoid_limits.svg");
}
```
