Nonempty finite centers, positive finite radii, nonnegative iteration
caps, nonnegative finite tolerances and nonempty callbacks are required.
Every returned gradient is checked for exact dimension and finite values
before indexing. Nonfinite objectives are rejected. Pure-cut states
must contain a finite square lower factor, stored zeros above it and
strictly positive diagonal. The two-dimensional outline helper validates
its sampling quota and returns an exactly closed native path.

A factor can represent a very large initial radius without first
squaring it. Later cuts may still overflow, and a positive support or
factor diagonal can underflow. These are explicit arithmetic errors;
a nonzero support is never silently treated as a zero subgradient.
When every proposed center coordinate rounds unchanged, the detailed
outcome reports center_stagnation, with no convergence claim. The
legacy ellipsoid_minimize keeps its original result layout and returns
the best finite evaluated point; use the work/trace outcome when the
reason for stopping matters. Correct zero-subgradient and one-dimensional
objective-unit stopping intentionally change some legacy callback counts.
The old one-dimensional loop evaluated the initial midpoint twice and
omitted the last cut center; the shared kernel evaluates sample zero
once and every accepted cut center, consistently in every dimension.
For the same finite cap, that newly evaluated center can improve the
returned one-dimensional best value.

The native tests compare random cuts against an independent extended
matrix calculation and check retained-half membership, exact volume
ratios, large/small positive gradient rescalings, original two- and
three-dimensional quadratics, nonsmooth/constant objectives, actual
callback sequences, history ownership, quotas and finite limits. Frozen
old/new programs compare 196 original scenarios, recording numerical
rounding and intentional stopping changes rather than promising bitwise
identity. This combination checks the implementation and states the
limits of what floating geometry can establish.

> Reference

S. Boyd (2018), Ellipsoid Method, Stanford EE364b lecture notes,
https://web.stanford.edu/class/ee364b/lectures/ellipsoid_method_notes.pdf.
The central-cut formula, one-dimensional bisection, conditional support
gap and volume argument follow these notes. The stored triangular
factor, explicit numerical stops and bounded actual-history API are
datamunge implementation choices.
